    "q-32969": {
      "dom_id": "step-q32969",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 3}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-2)\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-2)\\,i+\\frac{t-{t}^{2}}{1-t}\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to 3}\\sqrt{t})\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to 3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-2))\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to 3}t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2)\\,j+3\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\,i+0\\,j+3\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\,i+3\\,k \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}g(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to 3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-2))\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{t-{t}^{2}}{1-t})\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to 3}2)\\,k \\\\  & =({lim}_{t\\to 3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-2))\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{t(1-t)}{1-t})\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to 3}2)\\,k \\\\  & =({lim}_{t\\to 3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-2))\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to 3}t)\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to 3}2)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2)\\,i+3\\,j+2\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+3\\,j+2\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 3}g(t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+3\\,k)\\cdot (3\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
