    "q-69731": {
      "dom_id": "step-q69731",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+2g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+4}{t-2}\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2t-2 \\\\ \\frac{4t-1}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {4}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the limit of $f(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}2{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+4}{t-2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{0} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+4}{0-2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit of a second component here was found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{t} & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′}}{{t}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limit of $g(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}(2t-2) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{4t-1}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{4}^{t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(0)-2 \\\\ \\frac{4(0)-1}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {4}^{0}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+2g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
