      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}||f(s)||$, if $f(s)=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{s}^{2}}{s-1},\\frac{6s-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(s-1)}{3s-2},\\frac{{s}^{3}+4s-2}{{s}^{2}+s-1}⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s) & =⟨{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{s}^{2}}{s-1},\\,{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{6s-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(s-1)}{3s-2},\\,{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{s}^{3}+4s-2}{{s}^{2}+s-1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- The first component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{s}^{2}}{s-1} & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡s}{s-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡s)^{′}}{(s-1)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{(\\frac{2}{s})}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{6s-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(s-1)}{3s-2} & =\\frac{6(1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-1)}{3(1)-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-0}{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{s}^{3}+4s-2}{{s}^{2}+s-1} & =\\frac{(1)^{3}+4(1)-2}{(1)^{2}+(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=⟨2,\\,6,\\,3⟩.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖⟨2,\\,6,\\,3⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+36+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
