    "q-130289": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130289",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-2g(t)]$ given that\n$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2}-1,\\,\\frac{4+t}{2-{t}^{2}}⟩, \\\\ g(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\sqrt{t}+\\sqrt{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,({t}^{2}-1),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{4+t}{2-{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,{0}^{2}-1,\\frac{4+0}{2-{0}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(\\sqrt{t}+\\sqrt{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\frac{1}{2},\\sqrt{0}+\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{2},\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-2g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 0}2g(t) \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)-2{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩-2⟨1,\\frac{1}{2},\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩-⟨2,1,2\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-2,-1-1,2-2\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,2-2\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
